<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Lorsch</h1><p class="st-meta">764 CE – 1232 CE · capital Lorsch</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A royal Carolingian abbey near Worms, famous for its gatehouse, its library and the Lorsch Codex, a vast record of medieval land grants.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a> was founded in 764 by the Frankish count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cancor">Cancor</a> and his mother Williswinda, and handed to Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrodegang-of-metz">Chrodegang of Metz</a>. Relics of the Roman martyr <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-nazarius">Saint Nazarius</a> drew pilgrims and gifts. In 772 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> made it a royal abbey, giving it imperial protection and immunity.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a> grew into one of the richest abbeys of the Frankish realm, with thousands of donations recorded in the 12th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch-codex">Lorsch Codex</a>. Its library held important classical and Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch-pharmacopoeia">Lorsch Pharmacopoeia</a> is a key early medical text. The East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-younger">Louis the Younger</a> were buried there.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Decline came in the 12th century with disputes over its lands. In 1232 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Emperor Frederick II</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorsch">Lorsch</a> to the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, ending its independence; the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate</a> later took control. Its gatehouse, the Königshalle, survives and is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-lorsch.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Lorsch" width="320" height="272"><figcaption>Image: Kuebi - Armin Kübelbeck · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kloster_Lorsch_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>764 CE – 1232 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lorsch</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Chrodegang of Metz, Richbod</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Old High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lorsch Abbey</dd></dl></aside></div>
